export const PROCESS_READING_GUIDES = [
  {id:'understanding-railway-handovers',domain:'processes',title:'Six boundaries hidden inside the word handover',summary:'Learn which subject, evidence and responsibility is changing before drawing a process.',sources:['PR01','PR02','PR03','PR04','PR05','PR06','PR07','D02','OC04','OC05'],sections:[
    {heading:'Start with the subject',text:'A train can be physically complete but awaiting a technical question. An asset can be in use while some project responsibilities continue. A fault can be acknowledged while the condition remains. These are useful starting points for reading railway processes: ask what state is actually being described and whose question it answers. The six maps in this guide are original learning structures. Each names a subject, representative contributors, information entering and leaving the conversation, and a question that prevents two different states being confused. They do not provide a railway’s approval workflow or operating instructions.',sources:['PR03','OC05']},
    {heading:'Configuration and change',text:'INCOSE’s public competency framework places identification, controlled change, status and audit within configuration management. The corresponding learning map asks whether the changed item and its records still agree. A fictional module with a different connector is a useful example: the hardware observation, interface definition and test subject must refer to the same intended configuration. The IRSE public fleet case abstract provides a railway example of the need to connect testing, configuration verification and return to operation. Only that abstract is used here; its detailed process is not copied. A decision about a proposed change and evidence about an implemented change remain separate questions.',sources:['PR01','PR02']},
    {heading:'Asset handover is a lifecycle interface',text:'ARTC describes readiness across a project, with operations and maintenance needs connected to commissioning and handover. Metrolinx’s public overview separately identifies in-service information, handover records and asset-register updates. These examples explain why the receiving organization matters. In the learning map, a recipient needs an identifiable asset, usable information and a clear account of remaining conditions. The diagram does not assign contractual ownership or appoint an approver. Its useful output is a better question: can the people who inherit the responsibility identify what they are receiving and where its supporting information will be maintained?',sources:['PR03','PR04']},
    {heading:'Worksite handback concerns a different boundary',text:'A worksite handback relates work-side information to the relevant operational-control arrangement. The public RailSafe example connects infrastructure condition, restrictions and communication with network control. The educational map stops at those relationships: it supplies no order for removing protection, restoring power, testing signalling or moving a train. Completing one discipline’s task can leave another interface unresolved. A work record, an understood restriction and an operational permission should therefore remain distinct in the reader’s mental model. Other organizations may use “handback” for different transfers, so the page states which meaning is being explored.',sources:['PR06','PR07']},
    {heading:'Maintenance delivery, fleet readiness and duty continuity',text:'ORR’s maintenance-function explanation distinguishes technical delivery and its release documentation from fleet-maintenance management’s return-to-operation function. The maintenance map follows that information towards a proposed allocation, while keeping the subsequent movement-control interface separate. Duty handover has a different subject again: people need continuity of task-relevant information. HSE describes preparation, two-way exchange and incoming cross-checking. In that map, an outstanding item remains outstanding after the duty holder changes. Neither a completed form nor a new person makes the physical condition disappear.',sources:['D02','OC04']},
    {heading:'Coordination continues while evidence develops',text:'Incident coordination brings reports and responsible teams together. It is often better understood through parallel interfaces than through a single row of steps. The map therefore separates operational, passenger and technical questions and brings their updates back to a shared record. Acknowledgement is attention; coordination is an organized relationship between responsibilities; resolution needs its own evidence. For a graduate entering the industry, follow a real component link from any map, then return and ask who needs its information, what the information establishes, and which decision is still outside that component’s responsibility.',sources:['OC05','OC03']},
  ]},
  {id:'people-automation-and-control',domain:'people',title:'People, automation and train-control families',summary:'Understand what GoA changes without confusing a supplier, a radio or a staffing model with an operating function.',sources:['S08','PR08','PR09','T01','S15','S16','S05','PR10','PR11','OC01'],sections:[
    {heading:'Begin with responsibilities',text:'The role cards describe functions that a railway needs to allocate, rather than an organization chart or a set of staffing numbers. A person may perform several functions; another railway may distribute the same functions across several teams or locations. Traffic coordination, signalling control, power supervision, passenger support and technical maintenance need different information and produce different outputs. Reading the boundary on each card is as useful as reading its responsibilities: a service plan, a received indication, a maintenance record and a movement authority do not establish the same thing.',sources:['OC01','O01','D02']},
    {heading:'GoA describes train-operating allocation',text:'The automation comparison follows the ERTMS Users Group’s public explanation of GoA1 to GoA4. Manual driving, automatic driving with a driver, driverless operation with an onboard operating role, and unattended capability describe different allocations. They do not identify one signalling product. In particular, GoA4 capability removes reliance on an onboard driver or operating attendant for the defined routine operation; it does not mean that every person disappears from the train or railway. Degraded operation, recovery and the boundary between operating modes still need defined arrangements.',sources:['S08','PR08']},
    {heading:'Passenger and field responsibilities remain visible',text:'ORR’s public GoA4 guidance addresses passenger communication, emergency interfaces, depot operation and control-centre responsibilities alongside automated train operation. A dated Community of Metros research summary also reports that some GoA4-capable metros chose to carry attendants for other purposes, including customer service. These sources support a careful distinction between technical capability and the chosen operating model. They do not support a universal claim that platform attendants will be removed or that a particular percentage of staff can be eliminated. Assistance, accessibility, local conditions and the railway’s responsibilities remain questions to examine.',sources:['PR08','PR09']},
    {heading:'Keep families, functions and suppliers separate',text:'The train-control map offers conventional signalling, CBTC and ETCS/ERTMS as parallel learning routes. Protection, automatic operation, supervision, route control and detection are functions that help explain those routes. ATC is an umbrella term whose exact use varies; it should not stand for one supplier. ETCS is not a subtype of CBTC, and ATO can be considered separately over ETCS. Supplier examples then show how functions are implemented: SelTrac, Trainguard MT and Urbalis are named examples, not interchangeable diagrams. Historical SelTrac material may name Thales; Hitachi Rail’s acquisition notice explains the current supplier lineage.',sources:['T01','S08','S15','S16','S05','PR10','PR11']},
    {heading:'A device can belong in several explanations',text:'A track circuit or axle counter answers a detection question. A balise or other reference device supplies information with a defined meaning in its system. A radio transports messages. Those equipment questions cut across the navigation tree, so links to shared devices should preserve the same component identity. Start with the family overview, inspect the relevant device, then return to the functional relationship. That route helps a newcomer understand why similar-looking hardware does not make two architectures equivalent.',sources:['S13','S14','S16','S18','PR10']},
  ]},
];
